  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "Cook quinoa: Put the quinoa in a sieve, then rinse well and drain (see Kitchen 101). Put in a small saucepan with 250ml (1 cup) water, cover and bring to a simmer. Reduce the heat to low and cook, covered, for 12-15 mins until the water is absorbed and the quinoa is tender. Turn off the heat and stand, covered, for 5 mins.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/3843755/photos/large/107528_1-c6d33abb69bbea99e2dad6d932719a80.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Prep ingredients: Meanwhile, pick the coriander leaves and finely chop the stems. Crush or finely chop 2 garlic cloves. Drain and rinse the chickpeas.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/3843758/photos/large/107528_2-b808c6222b1c0cdfecc2e51c0903f761.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Start tagine: Heat 1 tbs olive oil in a medium deep frypan over medium-high heat. Cook the coriander stems and garlic, stirring, for 1-2 mins until fragrant. Add the chickpeas, Moroccan seasoning and half the tomato paste and cook, stirring, for 1-2 mins until fragrant.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/3843761/photos/large/107528_3-ddc777d3fb8886906b5813e2c052ea3f.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Simmer tagine: Add the tomatoes, mango chutney, 2 tsp honey and 125ml (½ cup) water to the chickpea mixture and bring to the boil. Reduce the heat to medium-low and cook, stirring occasionally, for 12 mins or until slightly reduced. Remove the pan from the heat. Taste, then season with salt and pepper.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/3843763/photos/large/107528_4-b720471f00147b470d2d37e0cdc2a871.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Serve up: Divide the quinoa and tagine among bowls and top with the yoghurt and coriander leaves. Enjoy!",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/3843765/photos/large/107528_5-eeadb3091dc2beb9e1012e8c1569cfef.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Kitchen 101: It’s important to rinse the quinoa well to remove the natural coating called saponin, which can make it taste bitter or soapy.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/3843770/photos/large/107528_6-f523e05d68fc18f3c24b400cbadc3cf0.jpeg"
    }
  ]
}
